== 1.7 Chapter 1 Practice Drill — 30 Questions

30 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 33 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [A set-text question differs from an unseen question because it assumes],
  [that the text is poetry],
  [a longer extract],
  [that the evidence is already yours and asks you to select it],
  [a higher mark]
)

#mcq(2, [The “unit” of a set-text answer is],
  [the whole play],
  [the author's biography],
  [the genre],
  [the scene, chapter or speech the question is anchored to]
)

#mcq(3, [A question beginning “What is the function of …” asks for],
  [a summary],
  [the character's motives],
  [a relationship between the part and the whole],
  [the act number]
)

#mcq(4, [A question asking for “the effect of the repetition” wants],
  [the device's definition],
  [what the audience now understands, and the words that produce it],
  [the line numbers],
  [the number of repetitions]
)

#mcq(5, [A stem beginning “How far does the play persuade us …” requires],
  [a yes or no],
  [a claim, the strongest objection, and a verdict],
  [a character description],
  [a summary of the plot]
)

#mcq(6, [The four shapes of set-text question are fact, function, effect and],
  [opinion],
  [translation],
  [definition],
  [judgement]
)

#mcq(7, [A four-part set-text answer contains],
  [unit, name, evidence, effect],
  [author, date, genre, title],
  [name, definition, example, summary],
  [plot, character, theme, setting]
)

#mcq(8, [The most economical length for a quotation in an exam answer is],
  [a whole speech],
  [six to ten words],
  [one word],
  [twenty words]
)

#mcq(9, [A quotation should be],
  [repeated for emphasis],
  [embedded in the candidate's own sentence],
  [as long as possible],
  [displayed on its own line]
)

#mcq(10, [A sentence in an answer should never end on],
  [a quotation],
  [a semicolon],
  [a date],
  [a name]
)

#mcq(11, [If you are not certain of a line's exact words, the correct policy is],
  [quote it approximately and hope],
  [describe the line instead of quoting it],
  [invent a plausible line],
  [quote it twice]
)

#mcq(12, [“Iago is jealous of Othello” is a weak answer because it],
  [uses a character's name],
  [is true of the whole play and of no scene in particular],
  [misnames the play],
  [is untrue]
)

#mcq(13, [“Shakespeare uses dramatic irony here” is weak because it],
  [quotes inaccurately],
  [names the wrong device],
  [does not say who knows what and who does not],
  [is too short]
)

#mcq(14, [“Iago is an evil man and deserves punishment” fails as a literary answer because it],
  [is morally wrong],
  [misquotes the play],
  [is too long],
  [offers a moral verdict instead of a function]
)

#mcq(15, [The commonest error on the set-text block is],
  [retelling the plot],
  [moralising],
  [misquoting],
  [spelling character names wrongly]
)

#mcq(16, [A “fact” question is best answered with],
  [the name and the unit in as few words as possible],
  [a paragraph of context],
  [a quotation and its effect],
  [an evaluation of the scene]
)

#mcq(17, [The cheapest marks on the text block are usually],
  [essay questions],
  [comparative questions],
  [fact questions],
  [judgement questions]
)

#mcq(18, [Spending four minutes on one judgement question is a mistake because],
  [judgement questions are unfair],
  [the answer must be short],
  [the text is not prescribed],
  [each question is worth one mark and the time is needed elsewhere]
)

#mcq(19, [A quotation that can only answer one question is],
  [the safest choice],
  [the only kind worth learning],
  [essential],
  [a luxury, because a good one does several jobs]
)

#mcq(20, [The number of quotations a candidate needs for a play is roughly],
  [fifty],
  [two],
  [a hundred],
  [ten]
)

#mcq(21, [A summary is poor preparation for the text block because it],
  [reports events in order rather than what each unit does],
  [is inaccurate],
  [uses the wrong names],
  [is too long]
)

#mcq(22, [The clause that usually earns the mark in a set-text answer is the one that names],
  [the character's age],
  [the act and scene],
  [the author],
  [the consequence for the audience]
)

#mcq(23, [“In the play” is a weak anchor because],
  [the play is not named],
  [it is ungrammatical],
  [it does not locate the evidence],
  [it is too formal]
)

#mcq(24, [A judgement answer that gives only one side of the case],
  [is preferred by examiners],
  [is correct],
  [earns full marks],
  [earns at most half the mark]
)

#mcq(25, [The best order for working a set-text block is],
  [longest first],
  [fact questions first, judgement last],
  [in the order printed],
  [hardest first]
)

#mcq(26, [An answer that could be written about a different text has],
  [answered well],
  [misquoted],
  [used the wrong device],
  [failed to answer this one]
)

#mcq(27, [“The subplot makes the main marriage look like a choice rather than a fate” is],
  [a summary],
  [a judgement about the author],
  [a function claim],
  [a quotation]
)

#mcq(28, [The relationship between the set-text block and the unseen block is that],
  [the unseen block is easier],
  [they test different skills and should be revised together],
  [the set texts replace the unseen block],
  [only one appears in a paper]
)

#mcq(29, [A candidate who has read all five texts but cannot say what a scene does will probably score],
  [full marks],
  [in the forties],
  [in the seventies],
  [in the nineties]
)

#mcq(30, [The single most useful sentence to memorise for the text block is],
  [“This is a very good play.”],
  [“This shows that the theme is important.”],
  [“In this scene, … does …, which makes the audience …”],
  [“The author uses many techniques.”]
)

#shortcut-box("Before you turn to the key")[
  + Mark the whole set in *one pass* — every answer, right or wrong — before you read a single solution.
  + Write the score at the top of the page, because a drill you cannot compare with the last one tells you nothing.
  + Then read *every* solution, including the ones you got right: the second method is in the solution, not in the chapter.
]

